- If burn = $62,000/month, runway = $2,100,000 / $62,000 ≈ 34 months.
- If runway = 16 months (as you stated) then implied net burn = $2,100,000 / 16 = $131,250/month.
Because those imply different stories, confirm which is the correct net-burn figure to present.

2) Quick quantitative examples (how reductions affect runway)
If implied net burn = $131,250/month (16-month baseline):
- 10% cost reduction → new burn = $118,125 → runway ≈ 17.8 months (+1.8 months)
- 25% cost reduction → new burn = $98,437 → runway ≈ 21.3 months (+5.3 months)
- 40% cost reduction → new burn = $78,750 → runway ≈ 26.7 months (+10.7 months)

If net burn = $62,000/month (34-month baseline):
- 10% reduction → runway ≈ 37.6 months
- 25% reduction → runway ≈ 45.2 months
- 40% reduction → runway ≈ 56.5 months

One‑time cash actions (example):
- Immediate cash raise or asset sale of $500k buys you ≈ 3.8 months (if burn = $131k) or ≈ 8.1 months (if burn = $62k).

3) Cost-reduction levers (prioritized, with speed of impact)
High priority (fast, low friction)
- Freeze hiring and non-critical contractor spend (immediate). 
- SaaS consolidation and vendor renegotiation (30–60 days) — usually 5–15% savings.
- Cloud/infra optimization (rightsizing reserved instances, waste cleanup) — immediate to 60 days.
- Pause or re-scope marketing campaigns with low ROI — immediate savings and preserves high-ROI programs.

Medium priority (moderate impact, requires planning)
- Reduce discretionary travel/events and delay non-critical capital expenses.
- Re-negotiate leases or sublet unused office space.
- Optimize pricing / packaging to accelerate revenue or increase ARPU.
- Tighten collections and payment terms (AR) — speed cash conversions.

Higher impact (hard choices)
- Role-by-role headcount rationalization or selective furloughs (3–6 months lead time for impact on payroll).
- Temporary salary reductions or bonus deferrals (notify transparently).
- Strategic vendor consolidation or insourcing where cheaper.

