2) Runway scenarios (baseline + simple levers)
Assumptions: cash = $2.1M. Results rounded.

If burn = $62K/mo (current):
- Baseline runway = 2,100 / 62 ≈ 33.9 months.
- Reduce burn 20% → burn = 49.6K → runway ≈ 42.3 months (+8.4 months)
- Reduce burn 35% → burn = 40.3K → runway ≈ 52.2 months (+18.3 months)
- Reduce burn 50% → burn = 31K → runway ≈ 67.7 months (+33.8 months)
- Raise $1M → +1,000 / 62 ≈ +16.1 months → total ≈ 50.0 months
- Raise $2M → +32.3 months → total ≈ 66.2 months

If implied burn = $131.25K/mo (to match 16 months):
- Baseline runway = 2,100 / 131.25 ≈ 16 months (as stated)
- Reduce burn 20% → burn ≈ 105.0K → runway ≈ 20.0 months (+4 months)
- Reduce burn 35% → burn ≈ 85.3K → runway ≈ 24.6 months (+8.6 months)
- Reduce burn 50% → burn ≈ 65.6K → runway ≈ 32.0 months (+16 months)
- Raise $1M → +1,000 / 131.25 ≈ +7.6 months → total ≈ 23.6 months
- Raise $2M → +15.2 months → total ≈ 31.2 months

Takeaway: even moderate reductions (20–35%) materially extend runway when burn is high; fundraising at reasonable amounts also buys significant time but depends on market conditions and dilution.

3) Concrete runway-extension levers (with typical timing & expected saving range)
Quick wins (0–60 days)
- Pause hiring and freeze non-essential headcount (+ contractors). Impact: immediate; saves future FTE spend. Typical savings: 5–20% of burn depending on hiring plans.
- Pause discretionary marketing/travel/events and shift to performance marketing. Savings: 5–15% of spend; immediate.
- Vendor/SaaS rationalization and renegotiation (cancel unused seats, annualize discount). Savings: small to medium (5–10% of Opex) within 30–60 days.
- Cloud/infra optimization (rightsizing, reserved instances). Savings: 5–30% of hosting costs; 30–60 days to implement.

Medium term (1–3 months)
- Convert contractors to part-time / reduce contractor hours. Savings: 5–20% of payroll/contractor line.
- Renegotiate office lease / sublet or reduce footprint (if hybrid). Savings: medium; takes 60–90 days.
- Reduce non-critical projects, shift to higher ROI initiatives (product focus). Savings: reallocate resources; improves burn-to-growth ratio.

Bigger / strategic (3–6+ months)
- Headcount reductions (targeted or company-wide). Biggest single lever — large savings (20–50% of burn) but high impact on morale & delivery; requires severance planning.
- Pricing increases or product packaging changes to improve ARPU. Can materially reduce net burn-per-dollar-of-revenue if adoption is feasible.
- Strategic partnerships, channel deals, or reseller arrangements to accelerate revenue with low upfront cost.
- Short-term bridge financing (convertible note, VC bridge) or debt facility. Timing depends on investor appetite and covenants.

Alternative capital sources
- Equity raise (seed/series extension, VC, angels). Pros: non-interesting, cons: dilution and time required.
- Revenue-based financing or short-term debt (faster, but interest/covenants).
- Grants, R&D tax credits, advance payments from customers (if applicable).

